    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d605">

                <pb facs="0664" n="656" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg656"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.24 Raw Materials/69: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gordon</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">The
                                Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-04-12T15:00:00+00:20">April 12, 1939—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 12—12:01 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>37. The first Dutch press comment (in this morning’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Rotterdam Courant</hi>) concerning the reported Byrnes proposal<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d605-f1"/> for exchange of our surplus grain and cotton
                    against rubber and tin concludes as follows in translation: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“What many have for a long time thought they saw coming, namely the
                            disappearance of free capitalistic trade, now threatens to become a
                            reality.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gordon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d605-f1" n="1" xml:id="d605-f1-content">
                <p>James F. Byrnes, Senator from South Carolina; for text of Senator Byrnes’
                    proposal, see Department of State, <hi rendition="#italic">Press Releases</hi>,
                    April 15, 1939, p. 297.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
